              \.
          %h3#TheMotivicAspect The motivic aspect
          %p
            As our title indicates, it may be useful to note the analogy of the structures considered here to structures in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+cohomology"} motivic cohomology
            and in
            = succeed "-yoga." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/six+operations"} six operations
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motives+in+physics"} motives in physics
          %p
            First notice the following heuristics: Just as the idea of a category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motives"} motives
            is to constitute a “linearization” or “abelianization” of a category of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            is a process that sends (non-linear) spaces of field configurations to linear
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+quantum+states"} spaces of quantum states
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            may be added as elements of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            encodes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superposition+principle"} superposition principle
            and hence
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+interference"} quantum interference
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
          %p
