                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Fields}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne-Mumford+stack"} Deligne-Mumford stack
            (in the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gromov-Witten+theory"} Gromov-Witten theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+as+a+pull-push+transform"} pull-push quantization
            of a 2d field theory) the fact that pull-push quantization procceeds via morphisms of motives was amplified in (
            %a{:href => "#BehrendManin95"}> Behrend-Manin 95
            ), see also for instance the introduction of (
            %a{:href => "#Toen00"}> Toën 00
            ).
          %p
            Therefore it may be generally useful to refer to the process of quantization as discussed here as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motive"} motivic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
          %p
            In view of this, notice that in the context of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            (which is roughly an infinitesimal (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+geometry"}> formal
            ”) approximation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            as considered here) it is known that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+Galois+group"} motivic Galois group
            (namely the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck-Teichm%C3%BCller+group"} Grothendieck-Teichmüller group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "deformation%20quantization#MotivicGaloisGroup"} formal deformation quantization – Motivic Galois group action on the space of quantizations
          %h3#TheHolographicAspect The holographic aspect
          %p
            A priori, motivic quantization applies to
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theory"} topological field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theory"} boundary field theory
            (field theory with
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defect+field+theory"} defect field theory
            (field theory with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain+walls"} domain walls
            of arbitrary codimension). This induces boundary effects which are not purely topological but encode “physical” field theories.
          %p
            Concretely, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
