          %h3#AnomaliesAndOrientation Anomalies
          %p
            We see that the motivic quantization operation over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            depends on the existence of, and the choice of, an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation+in+generalized+cohomology"} orientation in E-cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly+cancellation"} quantum anomaly cancellation
            conditions.
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} physical theory
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomaly
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/obstruction"} obstruction
                  to
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation+in+generalized+cohomology"} orientation
                  for
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/push-forward+in+generalized+cohomology"} push-forward
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  (geo-)metric structure of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theory"} boundary field theory
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  choice of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation+in+generalized+cohomology"} orientation
          %h3#Supersymmetry Supersymmetry
          %p
            Ever since the recognition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetric+quantum+mechanics"} supersymmetric quantum mechanics
            in the 1980s, it is a familiar fact that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index+theory"} index theory
            is naturally formulated in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebra"} superalgebra
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+functions"} partition functions
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetric+quantum+mechanics"} supersymmetric quantum mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/push-forward+in+generalized+cohomology"} push-forward in generalized cohomology
            is what generalizes the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index"} index
            to the “relative case”, motivic quantization may be thought of as intrinsically based on indices/partition functions. Accordingly one may expect that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            plays not just an optional but an intrinsic role.
          %p
            Indeed, one can observe the following seemingly deep relation between supersymmetry and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher algebra
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed "-grading" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
                = succeed "-grading," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝕊
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝕊
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
                is the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere+spectrum"} sphere spectrum
            %li
              %p
                every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"} E-∞ ring
                is canonically
                = succeed "-graded," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝕊
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group+of+units"} ∞-group of units
                is.
          %p
            This is discussed in a bit more detail at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "super+algebra#AbstractIdea"} superalgebra – Abstract idea
          %p
            Accordingly, it follows that some kind of (“higher”)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            is intrinsic in motivic quantization.
          %p
            A basic example is given by 2-dimensional motivic quantization over
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KU"} KU
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group+of+units"} ∞-group of units
            is given, up to the 3-coskeleton, by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+super+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} smooth super ∞-groupoid
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+line+2-bundles"} super line 2-bundles
            (as discussed there). Accordingly, 2-dimensional local field theories, i.e.
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-models"} sigma-models
            are naturally refined to
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            = succeed "-models." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#ChargedParticleAtBoundaryOfSuperstring"} The charged particle at the boundary of the superstring
          %p
            But motivic quantization is not restricted to supersymmetric field theories, it is just that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+and+phase+space+in+physics"} higher phases
            are always naturally super-graded. Notably plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            encoded by traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic
            or generally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+spaces"} phase spaces
            is naturally subsumed. See below the example
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#PoissonManifoldAtTheBoundaryOf2dChernSimonsTheory"} The Poisson manifold at the boundary of 2d Chern-Simons theory
          %h2#ExpositionalSummary Expositional summary
          %p
            We give here a leisurely exposition of the main ideas of motivic quantization of local prequantum field theory. We follow (
            %a{:href => "#Nuiten13"}> Nuiten 13
            ) in outline. For more details see there and/or skip ahead to
            %em
              %a{:href => "#GeneralTheory"}> General theory
              \.
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#ExpositionIntroduction"} Introduction
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "ExpositionLocalPrequantumFieldTheory"} Local prequantum field theory
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#ExpositionLinearizationInGeneralizedCohomology"} Linearization in generalized cohomology
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#ExpositionCohomologicalQuantization"} Quantization by pull-push
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#ExpositionExamples"} Examples
          %h3#ExpositionIntroduction 1. Introduction
          %p
            Some words of context. See (
            %a{:href => "#Nuiten13"}> Nuiten 13, section 1
            ).
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            One of the modern cornerstones of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert%27s+sixth+problem"} Hilbert's sixth problem
            applied to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is the following definition:
          %p
            A
            = succeed "+" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theory"} topological
            = succeed "+" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theory"} boundary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defect+QFT"} defect
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field+theory"} local field theory
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-functor"} monoidal (∞,n)-functor
          .maruku-equation
