                    \\stackrel{C(-)}{\to}
                    KK_{bootstr}^{op}
                    \\hookrightarrow
                    \\left(Ho\left( KU Mod\right) \right)^{op}
                    \\,.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p
              The functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi DiffStack
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %msup
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mn 2
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi prop
                    %mo →
                    %mi KK
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(-) \colon DiffStack_{/\mathbf{B}^2 U(1)}^{prop} \to KK
              is discussed in (
              %a{:href => "#AlldridgeGiansiracusa06"}> Alldridge-Giansiracusa 06
              ,
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "#Nuiten13"} Nuiten 13, section 3
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#TuXuLG03"} Tu-Xu-LG 03
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi KK
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi KU
                    %mi Mod
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KK \to Ho(KU Mod)
              is due to (
              %a{:href => "#DEKM11"}> DEKM 11, section 3
              ). The statement about the factorization through the bootstrap category is (
              %a{:href => "amenable+topological+groupoid#Tu99"}> Tu 99
              ) combined with (
              %a{:href => "#DEKM11"}> DEKM 11, section 3
              ).
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              In particular, on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quotient+groupoid"} local quotient groupoid
              the functor of prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheSmoothKTheoryModelFunctor"}
              reproduces the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+groupoid+K-theory"} twisted groupoid K-theory
              defined via
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+vector+bundles"} equivariant vector bundles
              in (
              %a{:href => "#FHT"}> FHT, I, section 3.2
              ) and on general Lie groupoids the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+groupoid+K-theory"} twisted groupoid K-theory
              of (
              %a{:href => "#TuXuLG03"}> Tu-Xu-LG 03
              ).
          %h4#BeckChevalleyConditionForGroupoidKTheory
            Beck-Chevalley condition and functoriality of
            = succeed "-motivic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi KU
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KU
          %p Functoriality of
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Corr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mn 2
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi nice
                  %mo →
                  %mi KU
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Corr(\mathbf{H}_{/\mathbf{B}^2 U(1)})^{nice}
                  \\to
                  KU Mod
          %p
            requires that pullback in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+K-theory"} groupoid K-theory
            satisfies the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beck-Chevalley+condition"} Beck-Chevalley condition
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+product"} fiber product
          %p Here we list sufficient conditions for this to be the case
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                for untwisted correspondences of manifolds: (
                %a{:href => "#ConnesSkandalis84"}> Connes-Skandalis 84
                )
            %li
              %p
                for untwisted correspondences of equivariant manifolds: (
                %a{:href => "#EmersonMeyer08"}> Emerson-Meyer 08
                )
            %li
              %p
                for twisted correspondences of
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stacks+of+flat+connections"} moduli stacks of flat connections
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#FreedHopkinsTeleman07"} Freed-Hopkins-Teleman 07
          %p (…)
          %h3#dimension_3__pullpush_over__and_elliptic_genera
            Dimension 3 – Pull-push over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi tmf
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} tmf
            and elliptic genera
          %p
            (…)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
            (…)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-orientation"} sigma-orientation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+orientation+of+tmf"} string orientation of tmf
            (..)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+genus"} elliptic genus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten+genus"} Witten genus
            (…)
          %h2#Examples
            %strong III)
            Examples and applications
          %p We spell out and discuss examples and applications of the general method.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#ExamplesDimension1"} In dimension 1
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#ExamplesDimension2"} In dimension 2
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#ExamplesDimension3"} In dimension 3
          %h3#ExamplesDimension1 Dimension 1
          %h4#LagrangianCorrespondences Lagrangian field theory and prequantized Lagrangian correspondences
          %h5#idea_2 Idea
          %p
            Here we discuss the notion of
            %em prequantized Lagrangian correspondences
            and how it serves to embed traditonal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+mechanics"} Hamiltonian mechanics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+mechanics"} Lagrangian mechanics
            into the general context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+prequantum+field+theory"} local prequantum field theory
            and its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"} motivic quantization
          %p
            A traditional notion is that of a plain
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+correspondence"} Lagrangian correspondence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+submanifold"} Lagrangian submanifold
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            and another one, with opposite
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+structure"} symplectic structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+correspondences"} Lagrangian correspondences
            serve to encode
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphisms"} symplectomorphisms
            – hence transfomations between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+spaces"} phase spaces
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            – via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondences"} correspondences
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic manifolds
          %p
            But in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum field theory
            proper, and in particular with an eye towards
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantization"} prequantization
            of these symplectic manifolds by lifting them to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+circle+bundles"} prequantum circle bundles
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connection"} principal connection
            %em prequantized Lagrangian correspondence
            is the refinement of that of plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+correspondence"} Lagrangian correspondence
            which does properly respect and reflect this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantization"} prequantization
            information: a prequantized Lagrangian correspondence is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+subspace"} Lagrangian subspace
            as before, but now equipped with an explicit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformation"} gauge transformation
            between the pullbacks of the two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+circle+bundles"} prequantum circle bundles
            to the correspondence space.
          %p
            We discuss below how the concept of prequantized Lagrangian correspondences neatly unifies “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"}> classical mechanics
            ” formulated in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+flows"} Hamiltonian flows
            on them with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamilton-Jacobi+equation"} Hamilton-Jacobi equation
            for such flows.
          %p
            Then we show how also the notion of prequantized Lagrangian correspondence is – still naturally in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+prequantum+field+theory"} local prequantum field theory
            – further refined from the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic manifolds
            to that of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifolds"} Poisson manifolds
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+of+correspondences"} 2-category of correspondences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theories"} boundary field theories
            for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 2d Chern-Simons theory
            (a non-perturbative
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
          %h5#symplectic_manifolds_and_phase_spaces Symplectic manifolds and phase spaces
          %p
            We write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            with underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+2-form"} differential 2-form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ∈
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi cl
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \in \Omega^2_{cl}(X)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            this models the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mechanical+system"} mechanical system
          .num_example
            %h6#example_4 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
              describing the propagation of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              has as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo *
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2 = T^\ast \mathbb{R}
              and as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
              its canonical
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+functions"} coordinate functions
              on this phase space are denoted
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q,p \;\colon\; \mathbb{R}^2 \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}
              (called the “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+coordinate"}> canonical coordinate
              ” and the “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+momentum"}> canonical momentum
              ”, respectively), and in terms of these the symplectic form in this example is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                      %mo =
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi d
                      %mi q
                      %mo ∧
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi d
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega = \mathbf{d} q \wedge \mathbf{d} p
          %h5#symplectomorphisms_and_canonical_transformations Symplectomorphisms and canonical transformations
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              Given two
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic manifolds
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_1, \omega_1)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_2, \omega_2)
              (which might well be two copies of one single symplectic manifold), a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphism"} symplectomorphism
              between them
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \;\colon\; (X_1, \omega_1) \longrightarrow (X_2, \omega_2)
            %p
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \;\colon\; X_1 \longrightarrow X_2
            %p
              of the underlying
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+of+differential+forms"} pullback
              of the second
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
              along
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              equals the first,
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f^\ast \omega_2 = \omega_1
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_5 Remark
            %p
              In
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphisms"} symplectomorphisms
              are traditionally known as
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+transformations"} canonical transformations
              %em canonical transformation
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphism"} symplectomorphism
              of the special kind called a
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+symplectomorphism"} Hamiltonian symplectomorphism
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantization"} prequantization
          %p
            For the present purpose it is useful to formulate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphisms"} symplectomorphisms
            in the language of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+spaces"} smooth spaces
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics"} geometry of physics
            in the section
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "geometry%20of%20physics#SmoothSpaces"} Smooth spaces
          %p
            In terms of this there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+space"} smooth
            universal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi cl
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^2_{cl}
            of closed
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+2-forms"} differential 2-forms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            one such
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi cl
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \in \Omega^2_{cl}(X)
            is equivalently a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi cl
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\omega \;\colon\; X \longrightarrow \Omega^2_{cl}
          %p
            This is explained in detail at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics"} geometry of physics
            in the section
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "geometry%20of%20physics#DifferentialForms"} Differential forms
          %p In this language we have:
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_6 Remark
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphism"} symplectomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon (X_1, \omega_2) \longrightarrow (X_2, \omega_2)
              as above is equivalently a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi ω
                                    %mn 1
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↙
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi ω
                                    %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msubsup
                              %mi Ω
                              %mi cl
                              %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X_1 &amp;&amp; \stackrel{f}{\longrightarrow}&amp;&amp; X_2
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}_{\mathllap{\omega_1}}\searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow_{\mathrlap{\omega_2}}
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp; \Omega^2_{cl}
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              Yet another equivalent way to say this is that both
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_1, \omega_1)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_2, \omega_2)
              are naturally
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+topos"} slice topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi cl
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/\Omega^2_{cl}}
              and that a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphism"} symplectomorphism
              is equivalently just a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
              between them, in this
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+topos"} slice topos
          %h5#lagrangian_correspondences Lagrangian correspondences
          %p
            Since this makes the two symplectic manifolds correspond to each other, it is useful to express this as in the formal sense as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence"} correspondence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X_1 \to X_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence+space"} correspondence space
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+of+a+function"} graph
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi graph
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  graph(f) \hookrightarrow X_1 \times X_2
          %p
            or better as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence"} correspondence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi graph
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ×
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi p
                                  %mn 1
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↘
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi p
                                  %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; graph(f)
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; X_1 \times X_2
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{p_1}}\swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{p_2}}
                  \\\
                  X_1 &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; X_2
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Traditionally one considers now the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1 \times X_2
            itself as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            equipped with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+of+differential+forms"} pullback
            symplectic form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mn 2
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (X_1 \times X_2 , \; p_1^\ast \omega_1 - p_2^\ast \omega_2)
          %p
            and observes then that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            being a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphism"} symplectomorphism
            is equivalent to its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+of+a+function"} graph
            being a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+submanifold"} Lagrangian submanifold
            of this product symplectic manifold. In that case the above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence"} correspondence
            is called a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+correspondence"} Lagrangian correspondence
            between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_1, \omega_1)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_2, \omega_2)
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_7 Remark
            %p
              In the language of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+spaces"} smooth spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi graph
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} graph(f)
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotropic+subspace"} isotropic subspace
              equivalently means that there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+spaces"} smooth spaces
              of the following form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi graph
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi ω
                                    %mn 1
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↙
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi ω
                                    %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msubsup
                              %mi Ω
                              %mi cl
                              %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; graph(f)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow
                    \\\
                    X_1 &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; X_2
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}_{\mathllap{\omega_1}}\searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow_{\mathrlap{\omega_2}}
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp; \Omega^2_{cl}
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              This in turn is equivalent to being a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence"} correspondence
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+topos"} slice topos
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi H
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %msubsup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mi cl
                            %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/\Omega^2_{cl}}
          %h5#hamiltonian_correspondences Hamiltonian correspondences
          %p
            An important class of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphisms"} symplectomorphisms
            are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+symplectomorphisms"} Hamiltonian symplectomorphisms
            from a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            to itself, those which are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flow"} flow
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+field"} Hamiltonian vector field
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            induced by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+function"} Hamiltonian function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H \;\colon\; X \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{-,-\}
            induced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            and identifying the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivation"} derivation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi H
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{H,-\}
            with the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+field"} Hamiltonian vector field
            and the exponent notation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi H
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(t \{H,-\})
            with the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flow"} flow
            for parameter “time”
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in \mathbb{R}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi H
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp( t \{H,-\}) \;\colon\; (X,\omega) \longrightarrow (X,\omega)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Here we refer to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+correspondences"} Lagrangian correspondences
            induced from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+symplectomorphisms"} Hamiltonian symplectomorphisms
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %em Hamiltonian correspondences
          #HamiltonianCorrespondencesAreSpacesOfTrajectories.num_remark
            %h6#remark_8 Remark
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+space"} smooth
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence+space"} correspondence space
              of a Hamiltonian correspondence is naturally identified with the space of
              %em
                classical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectories"} trajectories
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Fields
                      %mi traj
                      %mi class
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi graph
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi H
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Fields_{traj}^{class}(t) = graph\left( \exp(t) \{H,-\}\right)
            %p in that
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  every point in the space corresponds uniquely to a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectory"} trajectory
                  of parameter time length
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
                  characterized as satisfying the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
                  as given by
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamilton%27s+equations"} Hamilton's equations
                  for
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi H
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              %li
                %p
                  the two projection maps to
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  send a trajectory to its inital and to its final configuration, respectively.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_9 Remark
            %p
              Forming Hamiltonian correspondences consitutes a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              from 1-dimensional
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordisms"} cobordisms
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+structure"} Riemannian structure
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+correspondences"} category of correspondences
              in the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+topos"} slice topos
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi H
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Bord
                      %mn 1
                      %mi Riem
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msub
                      %mi Corr
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\exp((-)\{H,-\}) \;\colon\; Bord^{Riem}_1 \longrightarrow Corr_1(\mathbf{H}_{/\Omega^2})
            %p
              since for all (“time”) parameter valued
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_1, t_2 \in \mathbb{R}
              we have a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
              (by
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+product"} fiber product
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondences"} correspondences
              exhibited by the following
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+diagram"} pasting diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi graph
                            %mrow
                              %mo (
                              %mi exp
                              %mrow
                                %mo (
                                %mrow
                                  %mo (
                                  %msub
                                    %mi t
                                    %mn 1
                                  %mo +
                                  %msub
                                    %mi t
                                    %mn 2
                                  %mo )
                                %mo )
                              %mrow
                                %mo {
                                %mi H
                                %mo ,
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %mo }
                              %mo )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi graph
                            %mrow
                              %mo (
                              %mi exp
                              %mrow
                                %mo (
                                %msub
                                  %mi t
                                  %mn 1
                                %mo )
                              %mrow
                                %mo {
                                %mi H
                                %mo ,
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %mo }
                              %mo )
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi pb
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi graph
                            %mrow
                              %mo (
                              %mi exp
                              %mrow
                                %mo (
                                %msub
                                  %mi t
                                  %mn 2
                                %mrow
                                  %mo {
                                  %mi H
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                  %mo }
                                %mo )
                              %mo )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msubsup
                              %mi Ω
                              %mi cl
                              %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; graph\left(\exp\left(\left(t_1+t_2\right)\right) \left\{H,-\right\} \right)
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp; &amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp; graph\left(\exp\left(t_1\right)\left\{H,-\right\}\right)
                    \&amp;&amp; (pb) &amp;&amp; graph\left(\exp\left(t_2 \left\{H,-\right\}\right)\right)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow &amp; &amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow
                    \\\
                    X &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; X &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; X
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \searrow &amp;&amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp;&amp; \swarrow
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; \Omega^2_{cl}
                    }
                    \\,.
          %h5#prequantized_lagrangian_correspondences Prequantized Lagrangian correspondences
          %p
            But the reason to consider
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+symplectomorphisms"} Hamiltonian symplectomorphisms
            instead of general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphisms"} symplectomorphisms
            is really because these give
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
            not just between plain
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %em prequantizations
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantization"} prequantization
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
              is – if it exists – a choice of
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connection"} principal connection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
              2-form is the given
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mo ∇
                    %mo =
                    %mi ω
