            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+geometric+quantization+of+2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} extended geometric quantization of 2d Chern-Simons theory
            (…)
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\pi)
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            of the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔓
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{P}
            is called the corresponding
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SymplGrpd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mn 1
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔓
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  SymplGrpd(X,\pi) \coloneqq \tau_1 \exp(\mathfrak{P})
                  \\,.
          %p
            If the canonical 3-class of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔓
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{P}
            is suitably integral, then this carries a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+2-bundle"} prequantum 2-bundle
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi χ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi SymplGrpd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mn 2
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\chi \colon SymplGrpd(X,\pi) \to \mathbf{B}^2 U(1)_{conn}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SymplGrpd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SymplGrpd(X,\pi)
            may be regarded as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+sectors"} instanton sectors
            of) the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 2d Chern-Simons theory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔓
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{P}
          %p
            There is a canonical inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi SymplGrpd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to SymplGrpd(X,\pi)
            and one finds that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi χ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi
            trivializes when restricted to this inclusion. Therefore the original
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            is a topological
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"} brane
            for the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 2d Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence"} correspondence
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↘
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi i
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ⇙
                            %mo ∇
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi SymplGrpd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %msup
                            %mi π
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mn 2
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi conn
                                %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; X
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{i}}
                  \\\
                  \\ast &amp;&amp; \swArrow_\nabla &amp;&amp; SymplGrpd(X,\pi^{-1})
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B}^2 U(1)_{conn^1}
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            is hence a kind or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+bundle"} prequantum bundle
            applicable to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifolds"} Poisson manifolds
          %p
            Therefore we can quantize the original Poisson manifold by applying motivic quantization to this boundary condition for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 2d Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
          %p
            We discuss this now for some cases of interest. First we show how this reproduces and refines traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+method"} orbit method
            for the construciton of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+representation"} irreducible representation
            of Lie groups from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coadjoint+orbits"} coadjoint orbits
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#GeometricQuantizationOfSymplecticManifolds"} Geometric quantization of symplectic manifolds
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a{:href => "#QuantizationOfLiePoissonStructure"} Quantization of Lie-Poisson structure
                \.
          %h5#GeometricQuantizationOfSymplecticManifolds Geometric quantization of symplectic manifolds
          %p
            We consider the special case of the
            %a{:href => "#PoissonManifoldAtTheBoundaryOf2dChernSimonsTheory"} above
            motivic quantization of Poisson manifolds where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\pi)
            happens to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega^{-1})
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_10 Proposition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
              is equivalent to the point
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SymplGrp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo *
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    SymplGrp(X,\omega^{-1}) \simeq \ast
                    \\,.
            %p
              Accordingly, the boundary condition for the Poisson
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 2d Chern-Simons theory
              induced by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
              which is symplectic is a correspondence of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ⇙
                              %mo ∇
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi B
                              %mn 2
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 1
                            %msub
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi conn
                                  %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; X
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow
                    \\\
                    \\ast &amp;&amp; \swArrow_\nabla &amp;&amp; \ast
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B}^2 U(1)_{conn^1}
                    }
            %p
              and so is equivalently given by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+bundle"} prequantum bundle
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi conn
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Ω
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mn 2
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi conn
                          %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\nabla \; \colon \; X \to \mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn} \simeq \Omega \mathbf{B}^2 U(1)_{conn^1}
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_13 Remark
            %p
              Traditionally the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+deformation+quantization"} strict deformation quantization
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
              with a good
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarization
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}
              is taken to be the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+operators"} compact operators
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/leaf+space"} leaf space
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi 𝒫
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X/\mathcal{P}
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids#EH"} EH 06
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+convolution+algebra"} groupoid convolution algebra
              of the symplectic groupoid of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega^{-1})
              naturally presented as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair+groupoid"} pair groupoid
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi SymplGrpd
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mi ω
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi Pair
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SymplGrpd(X,\omega^{-1}) \simeq Pair(X/\mathcal{P})
            %p
              However, once one passes from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair+groupoid"} pair groupoid
              from the point, since both are
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalent
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+operators"} compact operators
              is of course itself
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalent
              to the base algebra of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            %p
              As a consequence, the traditional description of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+deformation+quantization"} strict deformation quantization
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic manifolds
              in the refined perspective of (
              %a{:href => "geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids#EH"}> EH 06
              ) contains a conundrum: either one unnaturally breaks the natural Morita equivalence or else one arrives at a trivial quantization.
            %p
              In the following we see that this is resolved in motivic quantization. Here is is not just the symplectic groupoid itself that is quantized, but the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence"} correspondence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                    %mo ←
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi SymplGrp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast \leftarrow X \to SymplGrp(X,\omega^{-1})
              which exhibits the original symplectic manifold as a boundary of the corresponding
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 2d Chern-Simons theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SymplGrpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SymplGrpd(X,\omega^{-1})
              itself is trivial when properly regarded in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                    %mo ←
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi SymplGrpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast \leftarrow X \to SymplGrpd(X,\omega^{-1})
              is not and in fact yields the correct quantization.
            %p
              A conceptual way to understand this phenomenon is to recall that the symplectic groupoid of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
              is effectively a stacky model for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/leaf+space"} leaf space
              by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+leafs"} symplectic leafs
              of the Poisson manifold. Since for symplectic Poisson manifolds the leaf space is the point, accordingly so is the symplectic groupoid, up to equivalence.
          %p
            In order to motivically quantize, we need an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation+in+K-theory"} orientation in K-theory
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+polarization"} Kähler polarization
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega^{-1})
          %p
            One then observes that the push-forward of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            to the point in K-theory reproduces the traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega^{-1})
          %p
            We discuss now the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#QuantumStatesAndKTheory"} Quantum states and K-theory
          %p
            and then the
