                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; X//G
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{i}
                  \\\
                  \\ast &amp;&amp; \swArrow_{\nabla//G} &amp;&amp; \ast//G
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B}^2 U(1)_{conn^1}
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            The original
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation+in+K-theory"} orientation in K-theory
            extends to an orientation in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
            if it is itself equivariant, hence if the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+operators"} prequantum operators
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            respect the polarization. This is precisely the standard condition for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+operators"} prequantum operators
            to qualify as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+operators"} quantum operators
          %p
            Hence if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            carries a
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin%5Ec+structure"} spin^c structure
            then we have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/push-forward+in+generalized+cohomology"} push-forward in generalized cohomology
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mo !
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Rep
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  i_! \;\colon\; K(X//G) \simeq K_G(X) \to K_G(\ast) \simeq K(\ast//G) \simeq Rep(G)
          %p
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+induction"}> Dirac induction
            ”). Here we used on the left that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convolution+algebra"} convolution algebra
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X//G
            is equivalently the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+product+C%2A-algebra"} crossed product C*-algebra
            (as discussed there), and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Julg+theorem"} Green-Julg theorem
            which identifies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+K-theory"} operator K-theory
            of that with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\ast//G)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+ring"} representation ring
            of the group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p So the motivic quantization
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Rep
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  i_!(\nabla//G) \in Rep(G)
          %p
            of the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+action"} Hamiltonian action
            is an element in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Rep
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rep(G)
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
            equipped with the
            = succeed "-action" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %h5#QuantizationOfLiePoissonStructure Quantization of Lie-Poisson structures – The universal orbit method
          %p
            A particularly interesting case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic manifolds
            is the quantization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coadjoint+orbits"} coadjoint orbits
            of suitable
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+loop"} Wilson loop
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT+with+defects"} defect
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "orbit+method#GaugeAndGravityWilsonLoops"} orbit method – Nonabelian charged particle trajectories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+representations"} irreducible representations
            of these Lie groups, as such called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+method"} orbit method
          %p
            By the
            %a{:href => "#GeometricQuantizationOfSymplecticManifolds"} above
            discussion of the motivic holographic quantization of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coadjoint+orbit"} coadjoint orbit
            is naturally described by motivic quantization. But moreover, we discuss now how each such orbit boundary theory is naturally related by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT+with+defects"} defect
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 2d Chern-Simons theory
            which is given by the dual Lie algebra regarded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            with its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie-Poisson+structure"} Lie-Poisson structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
            that appeared as (
            %a{:href => "#FHT"}> Freed-Hopkins-Teleman, part II, theorem 1.28
            ): it is in the quantization language used here a “quantum operator” that sends boundary states of the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^\ast//G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 2d Chern-Simons theory
            to states of the coadjoint orbit, respecting the action of the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            = succeed "-2d" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^\ast//G
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+method"} orbit method
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            in that it contains the quantization of each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coadjoint+orbit"} coadjoint orbit
            to each representation as the quantization of one of its defects.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            be a Lie algebra. Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^\ast
            for its dual vector space regarded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            by its canonical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie-Poisson+structure"} Lie-Poisson structure
          %p The crucial fact that drives the following discussion is the following.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_12 Proposition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie-Poisson+structure"} Lie-Poisson manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^\ast
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^\ast //G
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coadjoint+action"} coadjoint action
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SymplGrpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    SymplGrpd(\mathfrak{g}^\ast) \simeq \mathfrak{g}^\ast//G
                    \\,.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "symplectic+groupoid#BursztynCrainic"}> Bursztyn-Crainic, example 4.3
            )
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_14 Remark
            %p
              The manifold of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathfrak{g}^\ast//G)
              in the standard presentation is
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo *
                      %mo ×
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^\ast \times G
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+bundle"} cotangent bundle
              of
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo *
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mo *
                    %mi G
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\mathfrak{g}^\ast//G)_1
                    \=
                    \\mathfrak{g}^\ast
                    \\simeq
                    T^\ast G
                    \\,.
          %p
            The canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+2-bundle"} prequantum 2-bundle
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi χ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mn 2
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi conn
                        %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\chi \colon \mathfrak{g}^\ast//G \to \mathbf{B}^2 U(1)_{conn^1}
          %p
            is given by the truncated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cohomology
            cocycle whose 1-form component is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Liouville-Poincar%C3%A9+1-form"} Liouville-Poincaré 1-form
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+bundle"} cotangent bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^\ast G
            under the natural identification
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mi G
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  ((\mathfrak{g}^\ast//G)_1 = \mathfrak{g}^\ast \times G \simeq T^\ast G
                  \\,.
          %p
            This trivializes along the canonical inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^\ast \to \mathfrak{g}^\ast //G
            and therefore we have a boundary condition
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi 𝔤
                            %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ⇙
                            %mi ξ
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi 𝔤
                            %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi G
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mn 2
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi conn
                                %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \mathfrak{g}^\ast
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow
                  \\\
                  \\ast &amp;&amp; \swArrow_\xi &amp;&amp; \mathfrak{g}^\ast // G
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B}^2 U(1)_{conn^1}
                  }
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ξ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi
            is a circle bundle with connection, the corresponding “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+bundle"}> prequantum bundle
            ”.
          %p
            Assume now that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            is semisimple with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+form"} Killing form
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle -,-\rangle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda \in \mathfrak{g}
            be a regular weight and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi λ
                  %mo ↪
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
